            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, a 10-year sealed battery alarm is better because it keeps working through power outages without any maintenance. Plug-in alarms with battery backup are fine, particularly for renters, as long as you replace the backup batteries yearly. A plug-in with a dead backup battery stops working during outages, when CO risk is highest."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do carbon monoxide detectors last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most CO alarms have a rated life of 7 to 10 years, set by the electrochemical sensor, not the battery. Sealed-battery models are designed to last 10 years. Every listed alarm gives an end-of-life signal, typically a distinct chirp pattern or display message, and the whole unit must then be replaced. Write the install date on the back."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a carbon monoxide detector if I have a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Portable generators are a major cause of CO deaths, and exhaust can enter through windows, doors and vents even when the generator is outside. Built-in CO shutdown on the generator does not protect the inside of the house. Keep working CO alarms on every level, and put a display alarm in the room nearest the generator."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What level of carbon monoxide sets off a detector?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "UL 2034 alarms must sound within 60 to 240 minutes at 70 ppm, 10 to 50 minutes at 150 ppm and 4 to 15 minutes at 400 ppm, and must not alarm at 30 ppm for 30 days. Low-level models like the Kidde COBDL10 add a separate alert below 30 ppm for households with more sensitive occupants."
          }
