      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A carbon monoxide detector is the cheapest piece of safety equipment in the house and the one most likely to be missing, expired or in the wrong place. Carbon monoxide has no smell or colour, and its early symptoms, headache, nausea and drowsiness, look like flu or a long day. The CDC puts accidental, non-fire CO poisoning deaths in the US at more than 400 a year, and they spike during winter storms and power outages, when furnaces run hard and portable generators come out. If you have a gas furnace, water heater, range, fireplace, attached garage or a generator, you need working CO alarms. This guide covers which ones to buy and where they go.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-co-alarms-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Carbon Monoxide Detectors Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide is produced whenever a fuel burns incompletely: natural gas, propane, oil, wood, charcoal, gasoline and diesel. In a house that means furnaces, boilers, water heaters, gas ranges and ovens, fireplaces, wood stoves, cars in attached garages and, during outages, portable generators. A properly working, vented appliance sends its CO outside. A cracked heat exchanger, a blocked flue, a backdrafting water heater or a generator running in the wrong place brings it inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            CO harms you by binding to haemoglobin far more readily than oxygen does, so blood carries less oxygen with every breath. Low levels over hours cause headaches, dizziness and nausea; high levels cause confusion, loss of consciousness and death, often in sleep. The danger is a function of both concentration and time, which is why CO alarms do not trip at a fixed number but respond faster as concentrations rise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Almost every home CO alarm sold today uses an electrochemical sensor. CO reacts at an electrode inside the cell and produces a small current proportional to concentration, which the alarm's circuit reads. These sensors are accurate, use little power and are specific to CO, which is why sealed 10-year battery alarms are possible. Their weakness is age: the chemistry in the cell degrades, so every CO alarm has a rated life, usually 7 to 10 years, after which it signals that it needs replacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            All six alarms on this list use electrochemical sensors and are listed to UL 2034. That baseline means the core detection is comparable across them. What separates them is power source, whether you get a numeric readout, whether they alert at low levels and whether they can trigger other alarms in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smoke alarms do not detect carbon monoxide. A standard smoke alarm, whether ionisation or photoelectric, senses particles, not gas. Unless an alarm is specifically labelled as a combination smoke and CO alarm, it offers no protection against CO, and plenty of homes discover that only after a near miss.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6 Best Carbon Monoxide Detectors, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We compared current alarms from First Alert, Kidde and X-Sense on UL 2034 listing, sensor type, power source, display and peak memory, low-level alerts, interconnect and smart-home features. Specs come from the manufacturers' product pages, spec sheets and Amazon listings; we have not lab-tested these alarms ourselves. We left out Google's Nest Protect because Google stopped making it in 2025 and now points buyers to First Alert's SC5, and we left out discontinued First Alert combination models still found at some retailers.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
