        <div id="ul-2034" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What UL 2034 Listing Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            UL 2034 is the US standard for single and multiple station carbon monoxide alarms. A listed alarm has been tested to sound within set time windows at set concentrations, and, just as importantly, not to sound at low background levels that would cause constant false alarms. Never buy a CO alarm without UL 2034 listing printed on the box or listing; cheap unlisted detectors sold online are a real problem in this category.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">CO concentration</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">UL 2034 alarm window</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it feels like</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Must not alarm for 30 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually no symptoms in healthy adults</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">70 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 to 240 minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Headache, fatigue over hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 to 50 minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Headache, dizziness, nausea</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 to 15 minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe symptoms, life-threatening within hours</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those windows are the key to understanding what a CO alarm will and will not do. A standard alarm will stay silent at 50 ppm for hours. That is deliberate: brief low-level readings are common near gas ranges and in homes on still days, and an alarm that sounded every time would soon have its batteries pulled. The standard is designed to protect healthy adults from acute poisoning, not to warn about chronic low-level exposure.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the digital display and low-level alert features matter. A display with peak memory lets you see a reading of 40 ppm that never triggered the alarm. A low-level alert model like the Kidde COBDL10 adds a separate warning below the UL 2034 alarm point. For households with infants, pregnant people, older adults or anyone with heart or lung disease, that extra sensitivity is worth having.
          </p>

          <p>
            The standard is periodically updated, and newer editions have tightened resistance to false alarms from common household gases and improved testing. When choosing between two otherwise similar alarms, a listing that names a recent edition of UL 2034 on the packaging is a small point in its favour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Plug-In vs 10-Year Sealed Battery vs Hardwired
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three ways to power a CO alarm, and the right one depends on where it goes and whether you are allowed to drill into the walls.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">10-year sealed battery</h3>

          <p>
            Sealed alarms like the First Alert CO710 and Kidde COBDL10 have a lithium battery built in for the life of the sensor. You install them, they run for ten years, and you replace the whole unit when it signals end of life. There are no batteries to change, no low-battery chirps and no temptation to borrow the battery for a TV remote. Because they do not depend on mains power, they keep working through outages, which is exactly when CO risk is highest. For most homes, they are the default choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug-in with battery backup</h3>

          <p>
            Plug-in alarms such as the First Alert CO615 run on mains power through a wall outlet and switch to batteries in an outage. They are good for renters and for quick installation, and they often include a display. The catch is the backup battery: if it is dead, the alarm goes silent during an outage, which is precisely when a generator or gas oven might be running. Replace the backup batteries every year, on the same day you change the clocks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardwired</h3>

          <p>
            Hardwired alarms connect to a 120V circuit with a battery backup and, usually, an interconnect wire to other alarms. New construction in many jurisdictions requires hardwired, interconnected smoke and CO alarms. If your house already has hardwired alarm wiring, replacing like for like with combination smoke and CO units is often the cleanest upgrade. The First Alert SC5 is also sold in a hardwired version for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical mix for many homes: sealed 10-year combination alarms outside bedrooms, a sealed 10-year CO alarm with display near the furnace or water heater, and a plug-in with display in a hallway or family room where you will actually see the numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="display" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why a Digital Display and Peak Memory Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            An alarm without a display tells you one thing: CO has crossed the alarm threshold. An alarm with a display tells you what the level is now, and peak memory tells you the highest level it saw since the last reset. That is useful in three situations, and they come up more often than people expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, diagnosis. If someone in the house has recurring headaches that ease when they leave, a peak reading of 30 or 40 ppm on a CO alarm near the furnace is a strong reason to have a technician check the heat exchanger and flue, even though the alarm never sounded. Without a display, that clue is lost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, after an alarm. When a CO alarm goes off and you have evacuated, the fire department or gas utility will want to know what happened. A peak reading tells them how high the level got. It also helps you judge whether a single brief spike from a gas oven warming up is a different situation from a sustained leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, generator nights. During a power outage with a generator running outside, a display alarm near the doors and windows closest to the generator shows whether exhaust is getting in, well before it reaches alarm levels. Seeing 10 or 15 ppm creep up is the cue to move the generator further away or reorient its exhaust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Displays typically begin showing readings somewhere above zero rather than showing every trace of CO, so a reading of zero means low, not none. Check the manual for the display's range. The CO710, CO615, COBDL10 and X-Sense SC07-W all include displays; the PRC710 does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Put Carbon Monoxide Detectors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Placement guidance comes from NFPA, whose CO alarm requirements were long published as NFPA 720 and are now part of NFPA 72, from building codes and from each alarm's manual. The core of NFPA's guidance is a CO alarm outside each separate sleeping area in the immediate vicinity of the bedrooms, and on every level of the home including the basement. Many local codes also require one in any bedroom with a fuel-burning appliance, and near an attached garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow the manufacturer's instructions on mounting height. Carbon monoxide mixes with air rather than sinking or rising sharply, so CO-only alarms can be mounted on a wall, on a ceiling or plugged into an outlet, depending on the model. Combination smoke and CO alarms go where smoke alarms go, on the ceiling or high on a wall, because smoke rises.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Put the first alarm where it will wake you, not next to the furnace.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The most important CO alarm is the one outside the bedrooms, because CO deaths happen in sleep. A second alarm near fuel-burning appliances is a useful early warning, but it is the hallway alarm that saves lives.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Avoid a few spots that cause false alarms or slow detection. Manuals typically ask for some distance from furnaces, water heaters and gas ranges, because brief startup puffs of CO cause nuisance alarms. Keep alarms out of bathrooms and other humid areas, away from direct sunlight, and away from vents, fans and windows that dilute the air near the sensor. Do not install one inside a garage unless it is rated for it, as temperatures and car exhaust can exceed its design range.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical two-storey house with a basement ends up with four to six alarms: one outside each sleeping area, one on each level including the basement, and one near the furnace room. Mark each alarm's install date on the back with a marker so you know when its end-of-life date is approaching.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="interconnect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Interconnected and Smart CO Alarms
          </h2>

          <p>
            Interconnection means that when one alarm detects CO or smoke, every linked alarm in the house sounds. It is the most valuable feature in a multi-storey home, because an alarm in the basement near the furnace may not be loud enough to wake someone two floors up behind a closed door. NFPA and most modern codes require interconnected smoke alarms in new construction for the same reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three ways to get it. Hardwired interconnect uses a dedicated wire between alarms on the same circuit and is standard in new homes. Wireless interconnect, as on the X-Sense SC07-W, links alarms by radio, which makes retrofitting an older home simple, but locks you into one brand. Smart alarms such as the First Alert SC5 interconnect over Wi-Fi and their own radio link and add phone notifications.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smart alarms earn their price in specific situations: second homes, rentals you manage, households where an elderly relative lives alone, and homes where pets are left alone during the day. A phone alert that CO is rising at your parents' house, or at a cabin you are not at, is worth a lot. In a house where someone is always home and awake to hear an alarm, the money usually buys more safety as additional basic alarms.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own Nest Protects, they continue to work, and Google has said they will keep receiving updates, but they have a 10-year life and Google no longer makes replacements. The First Alert SC5 can join an existing Nest Protect network, so you can replace them one by one as each reaches the end of its life, rather than all at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Generators and Carbon Monoxide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable generators are among the leading causes of consumer-product CO deaths in the US, and the pattern is grimly consistent: a winter storm or hurricane knocks the power out, a generator is run in a garage, basement, porch or near an open window, and exhaust gets into the house overnight. The CPSC has warned that a single portable generator can produce as much carbon monoxide as hundreds of cars, and an open garage door does not reliably clear it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The CPSC's guidance is to run generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from windows, doors and vents. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> goes through the distances and the common mistakes, such as running it under a carport or beside a bedroom window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many newer portable generators include automatic CO shutdown systems, sold under names like CO Shield, COsense and CO Guard, that turn the engine off if CO builds up around the generator. These are worthwhile and are covered in our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generator</a> picks, but they protect the space around the generator, not the inside of your house. They do not replace CO alarms indoors, particularly in the rooms nearest to where the generator sits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before outage season, check every CO alarm in the house. Test them, check the end-of-life dates and replace the backup batteries in any plug-in alarms. Add an alarm with a digital display in the room nearest to where the generator will run, so you can see low readings creep up before they trigger an alarm. If you are choosing a backup power setup now, our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> compares portable, inverter and standby options, and a battery power station avoids indoor exhaust entirely for smaller loads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas ovens and grills are the other outage hazard. Using a gas oven or range to heat a home, or bringing a charcoal grill or camp stove indoors or into a garage, is a common cause of CO poisoning during winter storms. Keep emergency heat and cooking outdoors or electric, and keep a battery-powered radio for updates, as covered in our <a href="/best-emergency-radio/">emergency radio guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="combo" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Combination Smoke and CO Alarms vs Separate Units
          </h2>

          <p>
            A combination alarm puts a smoke sensor and a CO sensor in one housing. The appeal is obvious: the hallway outside the bedrooms needs both a smoke alarm and a CO alarm, so one combination unit covers both with one device, one battery and one end-of-life date. For most bedroom hallways, a combination alarm with a photoelectric smoke sensor is the efficient answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are reasons to keep separate CO alarms elsewhere. Smoke alarms belong on the ceiling or high on a wall, and they are prone to nuisance alarms near kitchens and bathrooms. CO alarms can go lower, and the most useful spots for a CO alarm, near the furnace, the water heater or the door to an attached garage, are not always good spots for a smoke alarm. A separate CO alarm with a display also gives you readings that most combination units do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other consideration is lifespan. Both sensor types in a combination alarm age, and the unit is replaced when either reaches end of life. With 10-year sealed combination units, that is simple. Mixing separate smoke alarms and CO alarms of different ages means more dates to track; writing the install date on each one solves that.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sensible approach: combination smoke and CO alarms, preferably interconnected, outside every sleeping area and on every level, then dedicated CO alarms with displays near fuel-burning appliances and in the main living space. That covers code requirements and gives you the information a display provides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alarm-sounds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What to Do When a CO Alarm Sounds
          </h2>

          <p>
            Treat every CO alarm as real. Get everyone, including pets, out into fresh air immediately, then call 911 or your fire department from outside. Do not open windows and stay to investigate, and do not go back in until emergency responders have cleared the house. If anyone has symptoms such as headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion, tell the dispatcher; CO poisoning is treated with oxygen and needs medical attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Learn the difference between the alarm and the other signals. Most CO alarms use a pattern of four quick beeps for a CO alarm, while a single chirp at intervals usually means a low battery on replaceable-battery units, and a distinct chirp pattern signals end of life. The manual, and often a label on the back, lists the patterns for that model. An end-of-life signal means replace the alarm, not just the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the house is cleared, have a qualified technician inspect every fuel-burning appliance, flue and chimney before using them again. Common causes include a cracked furnace heat exchanger, a blocked flue or chimney, a backdrafting water heater, an unvented space heater and a car left running in an attached garage. A peak reading from a display alarm helps the technician understand what happened.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, test alarms monthly using the test button, which checks the horn and electronics, and replace any alarm that reaches its end-of-life date. An alarm installed ten years ago and never checked is not protection. Put CO alarm checks on the same calendar as smoke alarm checks and the clock change, and they will actually happen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on Smoke Alarms for CO</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard smoke alarm does not detect carbon monoxide at all, whether ionisation or photoelectric. Unless the label says combination smoke and CO alarm and lists UL 2034, it offers no CO protection. Many households discover this only after a near miss, so check every alarm in the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Plug-In Backup Batteries Die</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CO risk peaks in power outages, when generators, gas ovens and fuel heaters come out. A plug-in alarm with a dead backup battery goes silent exactly then. Replace the backup batteries every year and test monthly, or choose a 10-year sealed battery alarm that keeps working without mains power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Only Putting One Alarm Near the Furnace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An alarm in the basement may not wake someone two floors up behind a closed door. NFPA guidance calls for a CO alarm outside each sleeping area and on every level. Add the furnace-room alarm as an early warning, not as the only alarm in the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping an Alarm Past Its End-of-Life Date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Electrochemical sensors degrade, and every CO alarm has a rated life, typically 7 to 10 years. When it signals end of life, replace the whole unit, not the battery. Write the install date on the back of every alarm so you know when replacement is due.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Generator's CO Shutdown Instead of Indoor Alarms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CO shutdown systems on generators protect the space around the generator, not the inside of your house. Exhaust can still drift in through windows and vents. Run generators at least 20 feet away, exhaust pointed away from the house, and keep working CO alarms indoors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

