        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should a carbon monoxide detector be placed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NFPA guidance calls for a CO alarm outside each separate sleeping area, in the immediate vicinity of the bedrooms, and on every level of the home including the basement. Add one near fuel-burning appliances and the door to an attached garage. Follow the manual's instructions on height and distance from appliances, and keep alarms out of bathrooms and direct sunlight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a plug-in or battery carbon monoxide detector better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a 10-year sealed battery alarm is better because it keeps working through power outages without any maintenance. Plug-in alarms with battery backup are fine, particularly for renters, as long as you replace the backup batteries yearly. A plug-in with a dead backup battery stops working during outages, when CO risk is highest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do carbon monoxide detectors last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most CO alarms have a rated life of 7 to 10 years, set by the electrochemical sensor, not the battery. Sealed-battery models are designed to last 10 years. Every listed alarm gives an end-of-life signal, typically a distinct chirp pattern or display message, and the whole unit must then be replaced. Write the install date on the back.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a carbon monoxide detector if I have a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Portable generators are a major cause of CO deaths, and exhaust can enter through windows, doors and vents even when the generator is outside. Built-in CO shutdown on the generator does not protect the inside of the house. Keep working CO alarms on every level, and put a display alarm in the room nearest the generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What level of carbon monoxide sets off a detector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                UL 2034 alarms must sound within 60 to 240 minutes at 70 ppm, 10 to 50 minutes at 150 ppm and 4 to 15 minutes at 400 ppm, and must not alarm at 30 ppm for 30 days. Low-level models like the Kidde COBDL10 add a separate alert below 30 ppm for households with more sensitive occupants.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a combination smoke and carbon monoxide detector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Combination alarms are a good choice for hallways outside bedrooms, where you need both smoke and CO protection. They cover both with one device and one replacement date. Keep separate CO alarms near fuel-burning appliances and in living areas, where smoke alarms are prone to false alarms and a CO display is more useful.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I do if my carbon monoxide detector goes off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Get everyone, including pets, outside into fresh air immediately and call 911 from outside. Do not stay to open windows or look for the source. Do not re-enter until emergency responders say it is safe. Then have a qualified technician inspect every fuel-burning appliance and flue before using them again.
              </p>
            </div>

